WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.155
Hi, I'm Ivy!

00:00:02.155 --> 00:00:05.325
Can a model shaped like Jev run inside your browser tab?

00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:05.902
It can.

00:00:05.902 --> 00:00:06.478
Here's how.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:14.072
The model is the easy part: an encoder, and a head with one output.

00:00:14.072 --> 00:00:15.756
The hard part is its guarantee.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.985
The tab pairs the context with each option.

00:00:22.985 --> 00:00:26.334
So every row carries its own full copy of the context.

00:00:26.754 --> 00:00:31.195
Each row runs through a small cross-encoder, on WebAssembly, or WebGPU if

00:00:31.195 --> 00:00:32.676
your browser has one.

00:00:33.096 --> 00:00:35.171
Out comes a short row of logits.

00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:38.135
Keep the entailment column, and each option has one score.

00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:40.214
Then a softmax across them all.

00:00:40.214 --> 00:00:43.256
Nothing in the code knows how many options there are: N

00:00:43.256 --> 00:00:44.363
is a loop bound.

00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:50.724
Jev keeps each option in its own row, so no option sees its neighbours.

00:00:50.724 --> 00:00:53.960
The price: the context is encoded again for every option.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:58.468
The other shape packs every option into one sequence and encodes once.

00:00:58.468 --> 00:01:00.852
Both open System One models chose it.

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:02.215
That's why they're fast.

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:08.099
With one tokenizer on identical content, that isolation costs six point two

00:01:08.099 --> 00:01:10.415
times the tokens at sixteen options.

00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:14.084
And the export changes N itself.

00:01:14.084 --> 00:01:18.118
The only open System One model with a browser playground outputs

00:01:18.118 --> 00:01:19.585
twenty-five logits, every time.

00:01:19.585 --> 00:01:22.153
The option count becomes a tensor shape.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.607
The hard part of running Jev in a browser is not the model.

00:01:26.607 --> 00:01:27.610
It is the guarantee.

00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:34.921
To recap: one row per option, a price in tokens for that

00:01:34.921 --> 00:01:38.028
isolation, and an export that turns N into a shape.

00:01:38.448 --> 00:01:40.505
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:40.505 --> 00:01:41.093
I'm Ivy.

00:01:41.093 --> 00:01:41.387
Bye!
